Phase 5: the rigged sample and the closing demonstration

All three clauses of the closing condition demonstrated from a copy of the
repository taken out of version control into an empty directory.

1. A character whose parts are marked: the arm rotates about its shoulder
   while the body travels. Watched frame by frame, not judged from one frame.
   The first attempt put the shoulder at the middle of the arm's top edge and
   watching it showed the limb swinging away from the body and reading as
   having come off; the pivot now sits at the corner that touches the torso.

2. A character with nothing marked, through the same code path, produces the
   dash it produced before - proven by every reference frame still matching
   rather than by inspection. One command serves both because the document
   declares what it is: a swing names a part, and a rig without that part
   swings nothing.

3. The rotation comparison reproduces from the clean copy with the same
   figures that chose the route, and the decision is in the stack document.

Also proven in the interactive application, not only in batch: the editor was
started on the rigged character with a script that loads the extension's own
command module and calls it. The result was a new layer of nine frames, a tag,
the original layer untouched, and the command's own message naming where the
parts came from. That route is now recorded in the tool documentation, because
driving the menu with synthetic input failed again and the failure turns out
to be narrower than first thought - pointer motion arrives and highlights the
entry under it, while the button press goes nowhere.

Gates: 206 unit tests, 71 runtime checks, both demonstrations matching all
twenty reference frames, linter and formatter clean across 52 files, core
layer clean, core coverage 100 percent across 11 files.
